  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and validate an integrated photoelectrochemical system in which boundary-layer shear flow in a tandem BiVO4 photoanode and Si photocathode architecture transports CO2 generated by in-situ acidification of seawater bicarbonate from photoanodes to downstream photocathodes, enabling direct ocean capture and conversion without external CO2 bubbling while reducing pH-related transport losses and improving solar-to-fuel efficiency and carbon-product selectivity.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Because PEC devices that use flowing seawater bicarbonate without external CO2 bubbling remain largely unexplored, and boundary-layer molecular-flux catalysis can continuously deliver CO2(aq) reactants to catalysts, a plausible research hypothesis is to test a boundary-layer-flow PEC route for direct seawater carbon conversion without external CO2 bubbling."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The evidence supports a general performance concern for static or conventional low-concentration PEC systems: reported devices have low STF efficiencies of 0.08%, 0.43%, and 0.63%, while diffusion and pH losses are identified as a common limiting pattern in low-concentration electrolytes."];
  "c5" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is static PEC with diffusion creating pH gradient/depletion, and if diffusion causes transport losses/low efficiency, then conventional static PEC has major losses and low performance, so conventional static PEC has major transport losses and low STF/selectivity."];
